/* ============================================================
   ML Revision Q&A — single source of truth for every page and the
   Q&A bank. Questions are the ones that come up again and again in
   ML / DL / LLM / RAG / ML-system-design interviews.
   m = module id, s = section (tab) id, imp = must-know.
   ============================================================ */
window.MR_QA = [
  /* ---------------- 01 · Generalization ---------------- */
  { m: 'gen', s: 'tradeoff', imp: true,
    q: 'Explain the bias–variance tradeoff.',
    a: '<p><strong>Bias</strong> is error from overly simple assumptions: averaged over many training sets, the model still misses the true pattern (underfitting). <strong>Variance</strong> is error from sensitivity to the specific training sample: retrain on new data and the model changes a lot because it fitted noise (overfitting). Expected test error \\(= \\text{bias}^2 + \\text{variance} + \\sigma^2\\).</p><p>Raising capacity (deeper trees, higher-degree features, bigger nets, less regularization) lowers bias and raises variance. You pick complexity that minimises validation error. One-liner: <em>"bias is being consistently wrong, variance is being inconsistently right."</em></p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'tradeoff', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you detect overfitting and underfitting, and what do you do about each?',
    a: '<p>Compare training and validation error. <strong>Underfitting</strong>: both high and close → add features or capacity, reduce regularization, train longer. <strong>Overfitting</strong>: training low, validation much higher → more data, augmentation, regularization (L1/L2, dropout, weight decay), early stopping, simpler model, bagging, fewer features.</p><p>Get the split and a simple baseline right first — "overfitting" is often leakage or a train/test distribution mismatch.</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'tradeoff',
    q: 'Does more data reduce bias or variance?',
    a: '<p><strong>Variance.</strong> With more samples the fit depends less on any one noisy point, so the spread of fits shrinks. Bias is a property of the model class: a straight line stays wrong on a curve however much data you add. Try it above: raise the training points at degree 12 (variance falls) and at degree 1 (nothing changes).</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'tradeoff',
    q: 'Which algorithms are high-bias and which are high-variance?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Higher bias:</strong> linear/logistic regression, Naive Bayes, shallow trees, k-NN with large k.</li><li><strong>Higher variance:</strong> deep decision trees, k-NN with k = 1, high-degree polynomials, large unregularized neural networks.</li></ul><p>Ensembles target one side each: <strong>bagging</strong> (random forest) averages high-variance trees to cut variance; <strong>boosting</strong> adds weak, high-bias learners sequentially to cut bias.</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'tradeoff',
    q: 'Huge neural networks fit training data perfectly yet generalise. Doesn\'t that break the tradeoff?',
    a: '<p>This is <strong>double descent</strong>: test error rises as the model approaches the interpolation threshold (just enough parameters to fit the data), then falls again in the heavily over-parameterised regime. Implicit regularization from SGD, architecture priors, data augmentation and early stopping pick "simple" interpolating solutions. The classical U-curve still describes the under-parameterised regime and is what interviews mean by the tradeoff — mention double descent as the nuance.</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'regularization', imp: true,
    q: 'L1 vs L2 regularization — what is the difference and when do you use each?',
    a: '<p><strong>L2 (ridge)</strong> adds \\(\\lambda\\sum w_j^2\\): shrinks all weights smoothly, keeps them non-zero, spreads weight across correlated features, has a closed-form solution. <strong>L1 (lasso)</strong> adds \\(\\lambda\\sum |w_j|\\): drives some weights exactly to zero, giving sparse models and feature selection; with correlated features it tends to pick one arbitrarily.</p><p>Use L2 by default and when most features matter a little; L1 when you expect few relevant features or need an interpretable sparse model; <strong>elastic net</strong> when features are correlated and you still want sparsity.</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'regularization',
    q: 'Why does L1 produce sparse weights but L2 doesn\'t?',
    a: '<p><strong>Geometry:</strong> the L1 constraint region is a diamond with corners on the axes; elliptical loss contours usually touch it first at a corner, where some weights are 0. The L2 ball is round, so the touching point is generically off-axis.</p><p><strong>Gradient view:</strong> the L1 penalty\'s gradient is a constant \\(\\lambda\\,\\text{sign}(w)\\) that keeps pushing even small weights all the way to zero; L2\'s gradient \\(2\\lambda w\\) fades as \\(w\\) shrinks, so weights get small but not zero.</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'regularization',
    q: 'Why should you scale features before using regularization?',
    a: '<p>The penalty treats every weight equally, but a weight\'s size depends on its feature\'s units. A feature in metres needs a weight 1000× larger than the same feature in millimetres and gets penalised far more. Standardize first so the penalty reflects importance, not units. (Tree models don\'t need this.)</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'regularization',
    q: 'What regularization techniques are used in deep learning?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Weight decay</strong> (L2; use AdamW for decoupled decay with Adam).</li><li><strong>Dropout</strong> — randomly zero activations in training, an implicit ensemble.</li><li><strong>Early stopping</strong> on validation loss.</li><li><strong>Data augmentation</strong> — flips, crops, mixup; often the strongest regularizer.</li><li><strong>Batch/Layer normalization</strong> — mild regularizing noise, mainly stabilises training.</li><li>Label smoothing, smaller architectures, transfer learning from pre-trained weights.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'curves',
    q: 'How do learning curves help you decide what to do next?',
    a: '<p>Plot train and validation error against training-set size. <strong>Both high and converged</strong> → high bias: more data won\'t help; add capacity or features. <strong>Large gap, validation still falling</strong> → high variance: more data or more regularization will help. <strong>Validation suspiciously close to zero</strong> → suspect leakage. This turns "should we label more data?" into an evidence-based decision.</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'curves',
    q: 'Training loss goes down but validation loss starts going up. What is happening and what do you do?',
    a: '<p>The model has started memorising the training set — overfitting. Stop at the epoch with the best validation loss (early stopping, restore best weights), then add regularization (dropout, weight decay, augmentation) or reduce capacity. Also check that validation data is from the same distribution and not tiny or noisy.</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'validation', imp: true,
    q: 'What is cross-validation and why use it? How do you pick k?',
    a: '<p>k-fold CV splits the data into k parts, trains on k−1 and validates on the held-out part, k times, and averages. You get a lower-variance estimate of generalisation than a single split, use all data for both training and validation, and see the spread (std) across folds.</p><p>k = 5 or 10 is standard: higher k means less bias in the estimate but more compute and correlated folds. Use leave-one-out only for tiny datasets. Keep a separate untouched <strong>test set</strong> — CV scores used for model selection are optimistically biased (use nested CV if you need an unbiased estimate).</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'validation', imp: true,
    q: 'What is data leakage? Give examples and how to prevent it.',
    a: '<p>Leakage is information available during training that won\'t be available at prediction time, so offline scores are inflated and production disappoints.</p><ul><li><strong>Target leakage:</strong> features derived from the label or recorded after the outcome.</li><li><strong>Train–test contamination:</strong> scaling, imputation or target encoding fit on all data before splitting.</li><li><strong>Temporal leakage:</strong> random splits on time-ordered data.</li><li><strong>Group leakage:</strong> the same user/patient in train and test.</li><li><strong>Duplicates</strong> across splits.</li></ul><p>Prevent it with split design that matches production, all preprocessing inside a <code>Pipeline</code>, a point-in-time feature audit, and suspicion of any single dominant feature.</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'validation',
    q: 'How do you cross-validate time-series data?',
    a: '<p>Never shuffle. Use forward-chaining (<code>TimeSeriesSplit</code>): train on an expanding (or sliding) window of the past, validate on the next block. Leave a gap between train and validation if features use rolling windows, so no window straddles the boundary. Evaluate on the most recent period last — that is closest to production.</p>' },
  { m: 'gen', s: 'validation',
    q: 'Why do you need train, validation AND test sets?',
    a: '<p>Training fits parameters. Validation chooses hyperparameters, features and models — and because you made many choices against it, its score is optimistic. The test set, used once at the end, gives an unbiased estimate of real-world performance. Reusing the test set for decisions turns it into another validation set.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 02 · Metrics ---------------- */
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'confusion', imp: true,
    q: 'Precision vs recall — explain with an example of when you would prioritise each.',
    a: '<p><strong>Precision</strong> = of everything I flagged, how much was really positive: \\(TP/(TP+FP)\\). <strong>Recall</strong> = of all real positives, how many I caught: \\(TP/(TP+FN)\\).</p><ul><li>Prioritise <strong>recall</strong> when missing a positive is costly: cancer screening, fraud, safety defects — false alarms get a cheap second check.</li><li>Prioritise <strong>precision</strong> when acting on a false positive is costly: spam filtering (losing a real email), auto-banning users, sending a costly intervention.</li></ul><p>They trade off through the threshold; state the cost of each error and pick the operating point, e.g. "max precision subject to recall ≥ 95%".</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'confusion',
    q: 'Why is F1 the harmonic mean and not the arithmetic mean?',
    a: '<p>The harmonic mean is dominated by the smaller value. Precision 1.0 with recall 0.01 gives an arithmetic mean of ~0.5 but an F1 of ~0.02 — correctly calling the model useless. Use \\(F_\\beta\\) to weight recall β times as much as precision (F2 for screening, F0.5 for precision-heavy tasks). F1 ignores true negatives, which is a feature for imbalanced problems.</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'confusion',
    q: 'What are Type I and Type II errors?',
    a: '<p><strong>Type I</strong> = false positive (rejecting a true null; a false alarm). <strong>Type II</strong> = false negative (missing a real effect). Lowering the threshold trades Type II errors for Type I. In A/B testing α is the Type I rate and power = 1 − Type II rate.</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'confusion',
    q: 'How do you choose the classification threshold?',
    a: '<p>Not 0.5 by default. On a validation set, sweep thresholds and pick by the business rule: a minimum recall or precision, maximum F1/Fβ, or minimum expected cost \\(c_{FP}\\cdot FP + c_{FN}\\cdot FN\\). Re-check it when the class balance shifts in production, and never pick it on the test set.</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'confusion',
    q: 'Macro vs micro vs weighted averaging for multi-class metrics?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Macro</strong>: compute the metric per class, then average equally — every class matters the same, so rare classes count. Use when minority classes matter.</li><li><strong>Micro</strong>: pool all TP/FP/FN — dominated by frequent classes; for single-label problems micro-F1 equals accuracy.</li><li><strong>Weighted</strong>: per-class metric weighted by support.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'curves', imp: true,
    q: 'What does ROC-AUC mean, and when would you use PR-AUC instead?',
    a: '<p>ROC plots TPR vs FPR across thresholds. AUC is the probability that a randomly chosen positive gets a higher score than a randomly chosen negative — a threshold-free measure of <em>ranking</em>. 0.5 is random, 1.0 perfect.</p><p>With heavy imbalance, FPR stays tiny even when false positives vastly outnumber true positives (the denominator is the huge negative class), so ROC-AUC looks great. <strong>PR-AUC</strong> (average precision) uses precision, which exposes those false positives; its random baseline is the positive rate. Use PR-AUC for rare-event problems: fraud, CTR, disease, retrieval.</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'curves',
    q: 'Your model has 0.99 ROC-AUC on a fraud dataset. Is it good?',
    a: '<p>Not necessarily. At 0.1% fraud, an FPR of 1% means ~10 false alarms for every true fraud — precision ≈ 9%. Ask for PR-AUC, precision at the recall the business needs, the alert volume operations can handle, and a check for leakage (0.99 is also a leakage smell).</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'curves',
    q: 'Can a model with a higher AUC be worse in practice?',
    a: '<p>Yes. AUC summarises the whole curve, but you operate at one threshold. Model A can win on AUC overall while model B is better in the low-FPR region you actually use (e.g. top 1% of alerts). Compare partial AUC or precision/recall at the operating point. AUC also ignores calibration.</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'imbalance', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you handle an imbalanced dataset?',
    a: '<ol><li><strong>Confirm and measure right:</strong> keep validation/test at real-world ratio; use PR-AUC, recall@precision, F1, cost — not accuracy.</li><li><strong>Algorithm level:</strong> class weights / <code>pos_weight</code>, focal loss.</li><li><strong>Data level:</strong> undersample the majority, oversample or SMOTE the minority — only inside training folds.</li><li><strong>Decision level:</strong> tune the threshold.</li><li><strong>Get more positives</strong> or reframe as anomaly detection when they are extremely rare.</li></ol><p>Note that re-weighting or resampling distorts probabilities — recalibrate if the probabilities are used downstream.</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'imbalance',
    q: 'Why is accuracy a bad metric for imbalanced data?',
    a: '<p>It is dominated by the majority class: with 1% positives, predicting "negative" always scores 99% while catching nothing. Accuracy also hides which error type you\'re making. Report the confusion matrix plus precision/recall or PR-AUC.</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'imbalance',
    q: 'What is SMOTE and what are its pitfalls?',
    a: '<p>SMOTE creates synthetic minority samples by interpolating between a minority point and one of its minority-class nearest neighbours. Pitfalls: applying it before the train/validation split leaks synthetic copies of validation points into training; it can create noisy points in overlapping regions; it works poorly in high dimensions and with categorical features; and it is often no better than class weights plus threshold tuning on modern boosted models.</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'regression',
    q: 'MSE vs MAE vs RMSE — when do you use each?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>MSE</strong>: squares errors, heavily penalises large ones; smooth and differentiable, so a common training loss. Minimiser = conditional mean.</li><li><strong>RMSE</strong>: √MSE, in the target\'s units — easier to explain, still outlier-sensitive.</li><li><strong>MAE</strong>: robust to outliers, every unit of error costs the same. Minimiser = conditional median.</li><li><strong>Huber</strong>: quadratic for small errors, linear for large — a compromise.</li></ul><p>Pick by the cost: if one big miss is much worse than several small ones (inventory stock-outs), MSE/RMSE; if outliers are noise, MAE.</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'regression',
    q: 'What does R² mean? Can it be negative?',
    a: '<p>\\(R^2 = 1 - SS_{res}/SS_{tot}\\): the fraction of the target\'s variance explained relative to always predicting the mean. On held-out data it is negative when the model is worse than the mean. It always rises when you add features on training data, so use adjusted R² or validation R² for comparisons, and note it says nothing about whether errors are acceptable in absolute terms.</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'calibration',
    q: 'What is model calibration and when does it matter?',
    a: '<p>A model is calibrated if, among cases predicted at probability p, a fraction p are positive. It matters whenever the probability itself is used: expected-value decisions (ad ranking by pCTR × bid, credit pricing), thresholds shared across segments, combining models. Measure with a reliability diagram, ECE, Brier score or log-loss; fix with Platt scaling or isotonic regression on held-out data. Calibration doesn\'t change ranking or AUC.</p>' },
  { m: 'metrics', s: 'calibration',
    q: 'What is log-loss and why do CTR models use it?',
    a: '<p>Log-loss (binary cross-entropy) \\(= -\\frac1n\\sum [y\\log p + (1-y)\\log(1-p)]\\) rewards confident correct probabilities and heavily punishes confident mistakes. It is a <em>proper scoring rule</em> — minimised by the true probabilities — so it measures calibration and discrimination together. Ads/CTR systems need accurate probabilities for auctions, so they optimise and report log-loss (often normalised against a baseline) alongside AUC.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 03 · Optimization ---------------- */
  { m: 'optim', s: 'gd', imp: true,
    q: 'What happens if the learning rate is too high or too low? How do you pick it?',
    a: '<p><strong>Too high:</strong> steps overshoot the minimum, loss oscillates or diverges to NaN. <strong>Too low:</strong> training is slow, can stall on plateaus and may settle in poor regions. Pick it with an <strong>LR range test</strong> (increase η exponentially over a few hundred steps, choose ~10× below where loss blows up), then use a schedule: warm-up for the first steps, then cosine or step decay. Re-tune when you change batch size (roughly linear scaling) or optimizer.</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'gd',
    q: 'Is gradient descent guaranteed to find the global minimum?',
    a: '<p>Only for convex losses (linear/logistic regression, with a suitable step size). Neural network losses are non-convex, but in high dimensions most critical points are <strong>saddle points</strong>, not bad local minima, and many minima generalise similarly. SGD noise, momentum and good initialisation help escape saddles; flat minima tend to generalise better than sharp ones.</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'gd',
    q: 'What are learning-rate warm-up and decay, and why use them?',
    a: '<p><strong>Warm-up</strong> ramps η from small to the target over the first steps: early gradients are large and Adam\'s second-moment estimates are unreliable, so a full-size step can destabilise training (standard for transformers and large batches). <strong>Decay</strong> (cosine, step, linear) shrinks η later so the model settles into a minimum instead of bouncing around it. One-cycle schedules do both.</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'optimizers', imp: true,
    q: 'Compare SGD, SGD with momentum, RMSprop and Adam. Which would you use?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>SGD:</strong> step against the mini-batch gradient. Simple, needs a tuned schedule, zig-zags in ravines.</li><li><strong>Momentum:</strong> accumulate a velocity (β≈0.9) so consistent directions accelerate and oscillations cancel.</li><li><strong>RMSprop:</strong> divide by a running RMS of gradients — per-parameter adaptive step sizes.</li><li><strong>Adam:</strong> momentum + RMSprop + bias correction; robust defaults (lr 1e-3/3e-4, β=(0.9, 0.999)).</li></ul><p>Default: <strong>AdamW</strong> for transformers/NLP and most new problems; SGD + momentum with a schedule is still strong for CNNs and sometimes generalises better.</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'optimizers',
    q: 'What is the difference between Adam with L2 regularization and AdamW?',
    a: '<p>Adding L2 to the loss makes the decay term pass through Adam\'s adaptive scaling, so parameters with large gradient history get <em>less</em> regularization — not what you want. <strong>AdamW</strong> applies weight decay directly to the weights (\\(w \\leftarrow w - \\eta\\lambda w\\)) separately from the adaptive update, which generalises better and is the standard for LLM training.</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'optimizers',
    q: 'Why does Adam need bias correction?',
    a: '<p>m and v start at zero, so early on they are biased towards zero (after one step with β₂ = 0.999, v is only 0.1% of g²). Dividing by \\(1-\\beta^t\\) removes that bias; without it the very first updates would be far too large or small.</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'batch',
    q: 'Batch vs mini-batch vs stochastic gradient descent — trade-offs?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Batch GD</strong>: exact gradient over all data; stable but one update per epoch and memory-heavy.</li><li><strong>SGD (B = 1)</strong>: very noisy, many cheap updates, poor hardware use.</li><li><strong>Mini-batch</strong> (32–1024): the standard. Vectorised on GPUs, gradient noise ∝ 1/√B, which also acts as a regulariser.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'batch',
    q: 'What happens when you increase the batch size a lot?',
    a: '<p>Each step is more accurate and throughput rises, but there are fewer updates per epoch, so you must raise the learning rate (linear scaling rule) with warm-up. Too large a batch reduces the helpful noise and can converge to sharper minima that generalise slightly worse; memory also caps it (use gradient accumulation to simulate large batches). BatchNorm behaves differently at very small batch sizes.</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'gradients', imp: true,
    q: 'What is the vanishing gradient problem, and how do you fix it?',
    a: '<p>Backprop multiplies the gradient by each layer\'s weights and activation derivative. If those factors are typically below 1 — saturating sigmoid/tanh (sigmoid′ ≤ 0.25), small weights, long RNN unrolls — the gradient shrinks exponentially and early layers stop learning.</p><p>Fixes: <strong>ReLU-family activations</strong>, <strong>He/Xavier initialisation</strong>, <strong>residual connections</strong>, <strong>BatchNorm/LayerNorm</strong>, <strong>LSTM/GRU</strong> gates (an additive memory path) for sequences, shorter paths (attention), and layer-wise pre-training historically.</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'gradients',
    q: 'What are exploding gradients and how do you handle them?',
    a: '<p>The opposite: per-layer factors above 1 make gradients grow exponentially, giving huge updates, oscillating loss or NaN — common in RNNs and very deep nets with poor init. Fixes: <strong>gradient clipping</strong> (by global norm, e.g. 1.0), smaller learning rate, proper initialisation, normalization layers, and checking for bad inputs (inf, unscaled features).</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'gradients',
    q: 'Why is ReLU preferred over sigmoid/tanh in hidden layers? What is a dead ReLU?',
    a: '<p>ReLU\'s derivative is exactly 1 for positive inputs, so it doesn\'t saturate and gradients flow; it is cheap and gives sparse activations. A <strong>dead ReLU</strong> is a unit whose input is negative for every example (often after a large update or a bad bias), so its gradient is always 0 and it never recovers. Leaky ReLU, ELU, GELU (transformers) or a lower learning rate avoid it.</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'gradients',
    q: 'Why does weight initialisation matter? Xavier vs He?',
    a: '<p>Initialisation sets the per-layer gain: too small and signals and gradients vanish, too large and they explode; all-zero makes every unit identical (symmetry is never broken). <strong>Xavier/Glorot</strong> uses Var(w) = 2/(n<sub>in</sub>+n<sub>out</sub>), suited to tanh/sigmoid. <strong>He</strong> uses Var(w) = 2/n<sub>in</sub>, compensating for ReLU zeroing half its inputs.</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'norm', imp: true,
    q: 'How does dropout work, and what changes at inference time?',
    a: '<p>During training each unit is zeroed with probability p, and survivors are scaled by 1/(1−p) (inverted dropout) so expected activations are unchanged. Units can\'t rely on specific partners, which reduces co-adaptation and approximates an ensemble of thinned networks. At inference dropout is disabled — <code>model.eval()</code> — and no rescaling is needed. (MC dropout keeps it on at inference to estimate uncertainty.)</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'norm',
    q: 'What does batch normalization do? How does it behave in training vs inference?',
    a: '<p>For each feature it normalises activations using the mini-batch mean and variance, then applies a learned scale γ and shift β. This keeps activation distributions stable, smooths the loss landscape, allows higher learning rates and adds mild regularization. <strong>Training:</strong> batch statistics, while updating running averages. <strong>Inference:</strong> uses those running averages, so outputs don\'t depend on the batch. Pitfalls: tiny batches give noisy stats; forgetting <code>model.eval()</code>.</p>' },
  { m: 'optim', s: 'norm',
    q: 'BatchNorm vs LayerNorm — why do transformers use LayerNorm?',
    a: '<p>BatchNorm normalises each feature across the batch; LayerNorm normalises across the features of each example. LayerNorm doesn\'t depend on batch size or other examples, works identically in training and inference, and handles variable-length sequences — so transformers and RNNs use it (often RMSNorm in modern LLMs, which drops the mean-centring).</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 04 · Classic models ---------------- */
  { m: 'models', s: 'linear',
    q: 'What are the assumptions of linear regression?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Linearity</strong> of the relationship between features and target.</li><li><strong>Independence</strong> of errors (no autocorrelation).</li><li><strong>Homoscedasticity</strong> — constant error variance.</li><li><strong>Normality</strong> of errors (needed for confidence intervals and p-values, not for the point estimates).</li><li><strong>No (perfect) multicollinearity</strong> among features.</li></ul><p>Check with residual plots, Q–Q plots and VIF. Violations hurt inference more than prediction; fixes include transforms (log), interaction terms, robust/weighted regression and regularization.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'linear', imp: true,
    q: 'Why use logistic regression instead of linear regression for classification?',
    a: '<p>Linear regression outputs unbounded values, not probabilities, and its squared-error loss is pulled around by points far from the boundary (even correctly classified ones), shifting the decision boundary. Logistic regression passes the linear score through a sigmoid to get \\(p\\in(0,1)\\), is trained with log-loss (convex, maximum likelihood under a Bernoulli model), and its coefficients are log-odds — \\(e^{w_j}\\) is the odds ratio for a one-unit change in \\(x_j\\).</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'linear',
    q: 'Why is log-loss used for logistic regression instead of MSE?',
    a: '<p>With a sigmoid output, MSE gives a <strong>non-convex</strong> loss with flat regions where gradients vanish for confidently wrong predictions. Log-loss is the negative log-likelihood of a Bernoulli model, is convex in the weights, and its gradient is simply \\((p-y)x\\) — large exactly when the model is confidently wrong.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'linear',
    q: 'How does multicollinearity affect linear models?',
    a: '<p>Correlated features make coefficients unstable: large variance, signs that flip between samples, and misleading importance — although predictions can still be fine. Detect with correlation matrices and VIF (> 5–10). Fix by dropping or combining features, PCA, or ridge regression, which shares weight among correlated features.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'trees', imp: true,
    q: 'How does a decision tree decide where to split? Gini vs entropy?',
    a: '<p>Greedily: at each node, try every feature and candidate threshold and pick the split that most reduces impurity (information gain for classification; variance/SSE reduction for regression), then recurse until a stopping rule. <strong>Gini</strong> \\(=1-\\sum p_k^2\\) is the chance of misclassifying a random sample labelled by the node\'s distribution; <strong>entropy</strong> \\(=-\\sum p_k\\log p_k\\) comes from information theory. They pick nearly the same splits; Gini is a bit cheaper (no log) and is the scikit-learn default.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'trees',
    q: 'Why do decision trees overfit, and how do you prevent it?',
    a: '<p>An unconstrained tree keeps splitting until every leaf is pure, carving out regions for single noisy points — near-zero training error, high variance, and unstable (a small data change can change the whole tree). Prevent with <code>max_depth</code>, <code>min_samples_leaf</code>/<code>min_samples_split</code>, <code>max_leaf_nodes</code>, cost-complexity pruning (<code>ccp_alpha</code>), or by ensembling (random forest, boosting).</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'trees',
    q: 'Pros and cons of decision trees?',
    a: '<p><strong>Pros:</strong> interpretable, no feature scaling, handle mixed types, non-linearity and interactions, fast inference, robust to outliers in features. <strong>Cons:</strong> high variance and instability, axis-aligned splits (poor at diagonal boundaries), greedy (not globally optimal), biased towards high-cardinality features in impurity-based importance, and they can\'t extrapolate beyond the training range.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'ensembles', imp: true,
    q: 'What is the difference between bagging and boosting?',
    a: '<table class="tbl"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Bagging</th><th>Boosting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Training</td><td>Parallel, independent models on bootstrap samples</td><td>Sequential; each model fixes the previous ensemble\'s errors</td></tr><tr><td>Base learners</td><td>Strong, low-bias, high-variance (deep trees)</td><td>Weak, high-bias (shallow trees, stumps)</td></tr><tr><td>Reduces</td><td>Variance</td><td>Bias (and variance)</td></tr><tr><td>Overfitting</td><td>Hard to overfit by adding trees</td><td>Can overfit — needs learning rate + early stopping</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Random forest, extra trees</td><td>AdaBoost, gradient boosting, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost</td></tr></tbody></table>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'ensembles',
    q: 'How does a random forest work? Why the random feature subsets?',
    a: '<p>Train many deep trees, each on a bootstrap sample of rows, and at each split consider only a random subset of features (√p for classification). Predict by majority vote / average. Averaging reduces variance by roughly the correlation between trees, so de-correlating them matters: without feature subsampling, every tree would split on the same dominant feature first and the trees would be nearly identical. Out-of-bag samples (~37% left out of each bootstrap) give a free validation estimate.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'ensembles',
    q: 'How does gradient boosting work? What makes XGBoost/LightGBM fast and accurate?',
    a: '<p>Start from a constant prediction. Each round, compute the negative gradient of the loss w.r.t. current predictions (for MSE, the residuals), fit a small tree to it, and add it scaled by a learning rate: \\(F_m = F_{m-1} + \\eta\\, h_m\\). It is gradient descent in function space.</p><p><strong>XGBoost:</strong> second-order (Newton) approximation, L1/L2 regularization on leaf weights, sparsity-aware splits, column/row subsampling, parallel split finding. <strong>LightGBM:</strong> histogram-based binning, leaf-wise growth, GOSS sampling — very fast on large data. <strong>CatBoost:</strong> ordered target statistics for categorical features. Tune learning rate, number of trees (early stopping), depth, subsampling.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'ensembles',
    q: 'Random forest or gradient boosting — which would you choose?',
    a: '<p><strong>Random forest</strong> when you want a robust baseline with little tuning, parallel training, and less risk of overfitting noisy labels. <strong>Gradient boosting</strong> when you need the best accuracy on tabular data and can tune with a validation set and early stopping. Also consider latency (boosted models are often smaller/faster), monotonic constraints, and how well the model handles drift.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'ensembles',
    q: 'How do tree models measure feature importance, and what are the pitfalls?',
    a: '<p>Impurity-based importance sums the impurity decrease each feature provides across all splits. It is biased towards high-cardinality and continuous features and is computed on training data. Prefer <strong>permutation importance</strong> on validation data (score drop when a feature is shuffled) or <strong>SHAP</strong> values for global and per-prediction explanations. Correlated features split importance between them in all methods.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'svm',
    q: 'Explain SVMs, support vectors and the kernel trick.',
    a: '<p>An SVM finds the hyperplane that maximises the margin between classes; the training points on or inside the margin are the <strong>support vectors</strong>, and only they define the solution. The soft-margin version trades margin width against violations via <strong>C</strong> (hinge loss). The <strong>kernel trick</strong>: the dual problem only needs dot products, so replacing them with a kernel \\(K(x,x\\prime)\\) (RBF, polynomial) gives a linear separator in an implicit high-dimensional feature space — non-linear boundaries without computing those features.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'svm',
    q: 'What do C and gamma control in an RBF SVM?',
    a: '<p><strong>C</strong>: penalty for margin violations — large C fits training points tightly (low bias, high variance), small C allows a softer, smoother boundary. <strong>γ</strong> (RBF width): large γ means each point has a tiny radius of influence (wiggly boundary, overfit); small γ gives smooth, almost linear boundaries. Tune both together on a log grid with CV, after scaling the features.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'knn',
    q: 'How does k-NN work and how do you choose k?',
    a: '<p>A lazy, non-parametric method: no training; to predict, find the k nearest training points (Euclidean, cosine, …) and take a majority vote or average, optionally distance-weighted. Small k → low bias, high variance; large k → smoother, higher bias. Choose k by cross-validation (odd for binary). Scale features first, and use KD-trees or approximate nearest-neighbour indexes for speed, since prediction cost grows with the dataset.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'knn', imp: true,
    q: 'What is the curse of dimensionality?',
    a: '<p>As the number of features grows: the volume of the space grows exponentially, so a fixed dataset becomes sparse; distances concentrate (nearest and farthest neighbours become almost equally far), breaking distance-based methods; and the number of samples needed to generalise grows exponentially. Effects: overfitting, slow search, meaningless similarity. Remedies: feature selection, dimensionality reduction (PCA, autoencoders, learned embeddings), regularization, and more data.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'knn',
    q: 'Parametric vs non-parametric models?',
    a: '<p><strong>Parametric</strong> models have a fixed number of parameters regardless of data size (linear/logistic regression, Naive Bayes, a fixed neural net): fast, need less data, but make strong assumptions. <strong>Non-parametric</strong> models grow with the data (k-NN, decision trees, kernel SVMs, Gaussian processes): flexible, fewer assumptions, but need more data and can be slower and overfit.</p>' },
  { m: 'models', s: 'knn',
    q: 'What is Naive Bayes and why is it "naive"?',
    a: '<p>A generative classifier using Bayes\' rule, \\(P(y\\mid x)\\propto P(y)\\prod_j P(x_j\\mid y)\\), assuming features are conditionally independent given the class — the "naive" part, almost never exactly true. It still works well for text (bag-of-words spam filtering) because it is fast, needs little data and ranks classes well, though its probabilities are poorly calibrated. Use Laplace smoothing to avoid zero probabilities.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 05 · Unsupervised ---------------- */
  { m: 'unsup', s: 'kmeans', imp: true,
    q: 'Walk me through the k-means algorithm.',
    a: '<ol><li>Choose k and initialise k centroids (k-means++).</li><li><strong>Assign</strong> each point to its nearest centroid.</li><li><strong>Update</strong> each centroid to the mean of its assigned points.</li><li>Repeat until assignments stop changing (or WCSS improvement is tiny).</li></ol><p>It minimises within-cluster sum of squares, converges in a few iterations, costs O(n·k·d) per iteration, and finds a local optimum — so use several restarts (<code>n_init</code>) and keep the lowest WCSS.</p>' },
  { m: 'unsup', s: 'kmeans',
    q: 'What are the limitations of k-means?',
    a: '<ul><li>You must choose k.</li><li>Assumes convex, roughly spherical, similar-sized and similar-density clusters (Euclidean distance).</li><li>Sensitive to initialisation, outliers (means get dragged) and feature scale.</li><li>Hard assignments only; numeric features only (use k-modes/k-prototypes for categorical).</li></ul><p>Alternatives: GMM (elliptical, soft), DBSCAN/HDBSCAN (arbitrary shapes, noise), spectral or hierarchical clustering.</p>' },
  { m: 'unsup', s: 'kmeans',
    q: 'What is k-means++ and why does it help?',
    a: '<p>An initialisation scheme: pick the first centroid at random, then each next centroid with probability proportional to its squared distance from the nearest centroid already chosen. Centroids start spread out, which avoids two centroids sharing one cluster, speeds convergence and gives an \\(O(\\log k)\\)-approximation guarantee in expectation. It is scikit-learn\'s default.</p>' },
  { m: 'unsup', s: 'choosek', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you choose the number of clusters?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Elbow method:</strong> plot WCSS vs k and pick the bend where adding clusters stops helping much.</li><li><strong>Silhouette score:</strong> average of \\((b-a)/\\max(a,b)\\); pick the k that maximises it.</li><li><strong>Gap statistic</strong> (compare with random data), <strong>BIC/AIC</strong> for Gaussian mixtures.</li><li><strong>Stability</strong> of clusters across bootstrap samples.</li><li><strong>Domain usefulness</strong> — can the business act on k segments?</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'unsup', s: 'choosek',
    q: 'k-means vs hierarchical clustering vs DBSCAN — when would you use each?',
    a: '<p><strong>k-means</strong> for large datasets with compact, spherical clusters when k is roughly known. <strong>Hierarchical</strong> for smaller datasets where you want a dendrogram, nested structure, or to choose k afterwards (O(n²)). <strong>DBSCAN/HDBSCAN</strong> for arbitrary shapes, unknown k and explicit noise/outlier labels — but tuning ε/min_samples is tricky with varying densities.</p>' },
  { m: 'unsup', s: 'choosek',
    q: 'How do Gaussian mixture models differ from k-means?',
    a: '<p>GMMs model the data as a mixture of Gaussians with their own means, covariances and weights, fitted with EM. They give <strong>soft</strong> (probabilistic) assignments and handle <strong>elliptical</strong> clusters of different sizes. k-means is the limiting case with equal spherical covariances and hard assignments. Choose the number of components with BIC.</p>' },
  { m: 'unsup', s: 'pca', imp: true,
    q: 'Explain PCA. What are principal components?',
    a: '<p>PCA centres (and usually standardises) the data, computes the covariance matrix, and takes its eigenvectors — the principal components — ordered by eigenvalue. PC1 is the direction of maximum variance; each next PC is orthogonal and captures the most remaining variance. Projecting onto the top k PCs gives the best k-dimensional linear approximation in squared-error terms. In practice it is computed with SVD of the centred data matrix.</p>' },
  { m: 'unsup', s: 'pca',
    q: 'Why must you scale features before PCA? How many components do you keep?',
    a: '<p>PCA maximises variance, so a feature measured in large units (salary in rupees) dominates one in small units (age) purely because of scale. Standardise first unless features share units. Keep enough components for a target explained variance (90–95% on the cumulative curve), at the elbow of the scree plot, or whatever maximises downstream validation performance.</p>' },
  { m: 'unsup', s: 'pca',
    q: 'When does PCA help, and when does it hurt?',
    a: '<p><strong>Helps:</strong> many correlated features, multicollinearity in linear models, speeding up training, denoising, visualisation, fighting the curse of dimensionality. <strong>Hurts:</strong> components are hard to interpret; it is linear and unsupervised, so a low-variance direction can be exactly the one that separates the classes (then LDA or supervised selection is better); and it adds leakage risk if fit on all data.</p>' },
  { m: 'unsup', s: 'pca',
    q: 'PCA vs t-SNE vs UMAP?',
    a: '<p><strong>PCA</strong>: linear, fast, deterministic, preserves global variance, can transform new data — good for preprocessing. <strong>t-SNE</strong>: non-linear, preserves local neighbourhoods, great 2-D visualisations, but slow, stochastic, distances between clusters and cluster sizes are not meaningful, and no native transform for new points. <strong>UMAP</strong>: similar local structure with better global structure, faster, can transform new data — popular for embedding visualisation.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 06 · Features ---------------- */
  { m: 'features', s: 'missing', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you handle missing values?',
    a: '<ol><li><strong>Understand why</strong> they are missing (MCAR / MAR / MNAR) and how much — per column and per row.</li><li><strong>Drop</strong> columns that are mostly empty and uninformative; drop rows only if few and MCAR.</li><li><strong>Impute</strong>: median (numeric, robust), most-frequent or an explicit "missing" category (categorical), KNN or iterative/model-based imputation when other features predict it, forward-fill for time series.</li><li><strong>Add a missing indicator</strong> — missingness is often predictive.</li><li>Or use models that handle NaN natively (XGBoost, LightGBM).</li></ol><p>Fit imputers on training data only, inside the pipeline.</p>' },
  { m: 'features', s: 'missing',
    q: 'What are MCAR, MAR and MNAR?',
    a: '<p><strong>MCAR</strong>: missingness unrelated to anything (random sensor drop) — deletion is unbiased. <strong>MAR</strong>: depends on other observed variables (younger users skip the income field) — model-based imputation using those variables works. <strong>MNAR</strong>: depends on the unobserved value itself (people with high income hide it) — imputation is biased; model the missingness (indicator features), collect more data, or use domain assumptions.</p>' },
  { m: 'features', s: 'encoding', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you encode categorical variables? What about high-cardinality ones?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>One-hot</strong>: nominal, low cardinality, linear models and NNs.</li><li><strong>Ordinal/label</strong>: ordered categories, or tree models.</li><li><strong>Target / mean encoding</strong>: high cardinality (zip codes, merchant IDs) — compute out-of-fold with smoothing towards the global mean to avoid leakage.</li><li><strong>Frequency/count encoding</strong>: cheap signal of popularity.</li><li><strong>Hashing trick</strong>: huge or open vocabularies, unseen values; accept collisions.</li><li><strong>Learned embeddings</strong>: IDs in deep models and recommenders.</li></ul><p>Plan for unseen categories at inference (an "other" bucket) and group rare categories.</p>' },
  { m: 'features', s: 'encoding',
    q: 'Why is naive target encoding dangerous?',
    a: '<p>Each row\'s encoding contains its own label, so the feature leaks the target — especially for rare categories, where the encoding is nearly the label itself. The model over-trusts it and validation scores are inflated. Fix with out-of-fold encoding (K-fold), smoothing towards the prior, adding noise, or CatBoost\'s ordered target statistics.</p>' },
  { m: 'features', s: 'scaling', imp: true,
    q: 'Which models need feature scaling, and which don\'t?',
    a: '<p><strong>Need it:</strong> anything using distances or dot products or trained by gradient descent — k-NN, k-means, SVM, PCA, regularized linear/logistic regression, neural networks. <strong>Don\'t:</strong> tree-based models (decision trees, random forests, gradient boosting) and Naive Bayes, because splits and per-feature likelihoods are invariant to monotonic rescaling. Fit the scaler on training data only.</p>' },
  { m: 'features', s: 'scaling',
    q: 'Standardization vs normalization (min-max) vs robust scaling?',
    a: '<p><strong>Standardization</strong> (z-score) → mean 0, std 1; the default for most models, not bounded. <strong>Min-max</strong> → [0, 1]; useful for bounded inputs (images, some NNs) but one outlier squashes everything else. <strong>Robust</strong> (median, IQR) → resistant to outliers. (Note "normalization" can also mean scaling each <em>row</em> to unit norm, e.g. for cosine similarity.)</p>' },
  { m: 'features', s: 'outliers', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you detect and handle outliers?',
    a: '<p><strong>Detect:</strong> visualise (box plots, scatter), IQR rule, z-score/modified z-score, model-based (Isolation Forest, LOF, DBSCAN noise), and domain rules. <strong>Then decide:</strong> data errors → fix or remove; genuine extremes → keep (often the signal, e.g. fraud). <strong>Mitigate:</strong> capping/winsorizing, log or power transforms, robust scalers and losses (Huber, MAE), tree-based models. Never remove outliers from the test set to make metrics look better.</p>' },
  { m: 'features', s: 'outliers',
    q: 'How do you handle skewed features?',
    a: '<p>Right-skewed features (income, prices, counts, durations) can be transformed with <code>log1p</code>, square root, or Box-Cox (positive values) / Yeo-Johnson (any sign) via <code>PowerTransformer</code>. This helps linear models, distance-based methods and neural nets; trees don\'t need it. For a skewed <em>target</em>, train on log(y) and invert predictions (remembering that exp of a mean log is a median, not a mean).</p>' },
  { m: 'features', s: 'outliers',
    q: 'What feature selection methods do you know?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Filter</strong>: correlation, mutual information, chi-square, variance threshold — fast, model-agnostic, ignore interactions.</li><li><strong>Wrapper</strong>: recursive feature elimination, forward/backward selection — use the model\'s score, expensive.</li><li><strong>Embedded</strong>: L1 regularization, tree importances, SHAP-based pruning.</li></ul><p>Do selection inside cross-validation (selecting on the full data leaks), and remove leaky or unavailable-at-serving features first.</p>' },
  { m: 'features', s: 'outliers',
    q: 'Give examples of useful feature engineering.',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Dates:</strong> hour, day of week, holiday flags, cyclical sin/cos encoding, time since last event.</li><li><strong>Aggregations:</strong> user\'s 7/30-day counts, averages, recency–frequency–monetary (computed point-in-time to avoid leakage).</li><li><strong>Ratios and interactions:</strong> price per sq ft, BMI, clicks/impressions.</li><li><strong>Text:</strong> TF-IDF, embeddings, length. <strong>Geo:</strong> distance to store, clustering.</li></ul>' },

  /* ---------------- 07 · Transformers ---------------- */
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'attention', imp: true,
    q: 'Explain self-attention. What are Q, K and V?',
    a: '<p>Each token embedding is projected by three learned matrices into a <strong>query</strong> (what I\'m looking for), a <strong>key</strong> (what I contain) and a <strong>value</strong> (what I pass on). Attention scores are dot products of one token\'s query with every key, scaled by \\(\\sqrt{d_k}\\) and softmaxed into weights; the output is the weighted sum of values. Every token can draw information directly from any other token in one layer, in parallel — unlike RNNs, which pass information step by step.</p>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'attention', imp: true,
    q: 'Why are attention scores divided by √dₖ?',
    a: '<p>If q and k have independent unit-variance components, \\(q\\cdot k\\) has variance \\(d_k\\). For large heads the scores get large, softmax saturates into an almost one-hot distribution, and its gradients become tiny, so training stalls. Dividing by \\(\\sqrt{d_k}\\) keeps the variance at about 1 regardless of head size.</p>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'attention',
    q: 'Why multi-head attention instead of one big head?',
    a: '<p>Each head has its own Q/K/V projections in a smaller subspace (d_model / h), so different heads can attend to different relationships at once — syntax, coreference, nearby tokens, delimiters. One head would have to average all of these into a single distribution. The cost is the same as one full-width head; outputs are concatenated and projected back.</p>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'attention',
    q: 'Why did transformers replace RNNs/LSTMs?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Parallelism:</strong> all positions are processed at once in training, instead of a sequential loop — far better GPU use.</li><li><strong>Long-range dependencies:</strong> any two tokens are one attention step apart, rather than O(n) steps through a recurrent state that forgets and suffers vanishing gradients.</li><li><strong>Scaling:</strong> they keep improving with more data and parameters.</li></ul><p>The cost: attention is O(n²) in sequence length, and they need positional information injected.</p>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'attention',
    q: 'What is the computational complexity of self-attention, and how is long context handled?',
    a: '<p>O(n²·d) time and O(n²) memory for the score matrix, for sequence length n. Approaches: <strong>FlashAttention</strong> (exact attention computed in tiles in on-chip SRAM, never materialising the n×n matrix — big memory and speed win), sparse/sliding-window attention, linear-attention approximations, state-space models, and for inference, GQA plus KV-cache paging and quantisation.</p>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'positional',
    q: 'Why do transformers need positional encodings? Compare sinusoidal, learned and RoPE.',
    a: '<p>Self-attention is permutation-equivariant — it has no notion of order — so position must be injected. <strong>Sinusoidal</strong>: fixed sines/cosines of different frequencies added to embeddings; no parameters, unique per position. <strong>Learned absolute</strong> (BERT, GPT-2): an embedding per position; can\'t extend beyond the trained length. <strong>RoPE</strong> (Llama and most modern LLMs): rotates Q and K by position-dependent angles so their dot product depends on relative distance; extends to longer contexts with scaling tricks. <strong>ALiBi</strong>: adds a linear distance penalty to scores.</p>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'positional',
    q: 'What else is inside a transformer block besides attention?',
    a: '<p>A position-wise <strong>feed-forward network</strong> (two linear layers with a GELU/SwiGLU, usually 4× wider — most of the parameters), <strong>residual connections</strong> around both sub-layers so gradients flow through deep stacks, and <strong>LayerNorm/RMSNorm</strong> (pre-norm in modern models for stability). Attention mixes information <em>across</em> tokens; the FFN transforms each token independently.</p>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'architectures', imp: true,
    q: 'Encoder-only vs decoder-only vs encoder–decoder — differences and use cases?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Encoder-only</strong> (BERT): bidirectional attention, masked-LM pre-training; classification, NER, embeddings, cross-encoder re-ranking.</li><li><strong>Decoder-only</strong> (GPT, Llama): causal attention, next-token prediction; generation, chat, agents, in-context learning — the dominant LLM design.</li><li><strong>Encoder–decoder</strong> (T5, BART): encoder reads input, decoder generates with cross-attention; translation, summarisation.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'architectures',
    q: 'How do BERT and GPT differ in pre-training?',
    a: '<p><strong>BERT</strong>: masked language modelling — hide ~15% of tokens and predict them from both sides (plus next-sentence prediction originally). It learns rich bidirectional representations but can\'t generate text left-to-right naturally. <strong>GPT</strong>: causal language modelling — predict the next token from the left context. Every token is a training signal, it generates naturally, and it scales into general-purpose LLMs.</p>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'architectures',
    q: 'What is causal masking and why is it needed?',
    a: '<p>In a decoder, position i must not attend to positions after i, otherwise during training it could copy the token it is supposed to predict. The mask adds −∞ to the upper triangle of the score matrix before softmax, so those weights become 0. It lets the model train on all positions of a sequence in parallel while matching left-to-right generation at inference.</p>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'kvcache', imp: true,
    q: 'What is the KV cache and what are its memory implications?',
    a: '<p>During autoregressive generation, the keys and values of past tokens never change, so each layer caches them; a new token computes only its own Q, K, V and attends to the cache. This turns per-step cost from re-processing the whole prefix into one token\'s worth of work. Memory = 2 × layers × KV heads × head dim × sequence length × batch × bytes — it grows linearly with context and concurrent users and often dominates GPU memory, limiting throughput. Mitigations: GQA/MQA, paged allocation (vLLM PagedAttention), FP8/INT8 cache quantisation, prefix caching, sliding windows.</p>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'kvcache',
    q: 'What are MQA and GQA?',
    a: '<p><strong>Multi-query attention</strong>: all query heads share a single K and V head — the KV cache shrinks by the number of heads, with some quality loss. <strong>Grouped-query attention</strong>: query heads are split into groups that each share one K/V head (e.g. 32 query heads, 8 KV heads) — most of MQA\'s memory saving with near-MHA quality. Used by Llama 2/3 70B+, Mistral and most modern LLMs.</p>' },
  { m: 'transformers', s: 'kvcache',
    q: 'Why is LLM decoding memory-bound, and what speeds it up?',
    a: '<p>Each generated token requires reading all the weights and the KV cache from GPU memory but does little arithmetic per byte, so bandwidth, not FLOPs, is the bottleneck. Speed-ups: <strong>batching</strong> many requests (continuous batching), <strong>quantisation</strong> (fewer bytes to read), <strong>speculative decoding</strong> (a small draft model proposes several tokens that the big model verifies in one pass), KV-cache optimisations, and tensor parallelism across GPUs.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 08 · LLMs ---------------- */
  { m: 'llm', s: 'tokens',
    q: 'How does BPE tokenization work, and what are its failure modes?',
    a: '<p>Start with a base vocabulary of bytes/characters; repeatedly count adjacent symbol pairs in the training corpus and merge the most frequent pair into a new token until the target vocabulary size is reached. At inference, apply the learned merges in order. Frequent words become one token, rare words become several sub-word tokens, and nothing is out-of-vocabulary.</p><p><strong>Failure modes:</strong> character-level tasks (counting letters, reversing strings), inconsistent number splitting (arithmetic), more tokens per word for non-English scripts (cost, context), sensitivity to leading spaces and casing, and odd "glitch" tokens that were rare in training.</p>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'tokens',
    q: 'Why does tokenization matter for cost, latency and context limits?',
    a: '<p>APIs bill per token, latency scales with output tokens, and context windows are measured in tokens. Roughly 1 token ≈ 4 characters ≈ ¾ of an English word, but code, numbers, JSON and many Indian languages take noticeably more tokens. Always measure with the model\'s actual tokenizer when budgeting prompts, chunk sizes and costs.</p>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'decoding', imp: true,
    q: 'Explain temperature, top-k and top-p sampling. How would you set them?',
    a: '<p><strong>Temperature</strong> divides logits before softmax: low T sharpens towards the most likely token (T → 0 ≈ greedy), high T flattens. <strong>Top-k</strong> samples only from the k most likely tokens. <strong>Top-p</strong> samples from the smallest set whose cumulative probability ≥ p, so the candidate set shrinks when the model is confident and grows when it isn\'t.</p><p>Extraction, classification, SQL/code and grounded RAG answers: T ≈ 0–0.3. Chat: T ≈ 0.7, top-p 0.9–0.95. Creative writing/brainstorming: higher T. Usually tune temperature <em>or</em> top-p, not both aggressively.</p>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'decoding',
    q: 'Greedy decoding vs beam search vs sampling?',
    a: '<p><strong>Greedy</strong> picks the top token each step — fast, deterministic, but can be repetitive and miss better overall sequences. <strong>Beam search</strong> keeps the b best partial sequences — good for translation/summarisation with a clear target, but tends towards bland, repetitive text in open-ended generation. <strong>Sampling</strong> (with temperature/top-p) gives diverse, natural text at the cost of determinism. Repetition penalties and stop sequences are common add-ons.</p>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'decoding',
    q: 'Why can the same prompt give different outputs even at temperature 0?',
    a: '<p>Floating-point non-determinism on GPUs (different batch compositions and kernel reduction orders change logits slightly), ties between near-equal tokens, mixture-of-experts routing, and provider-side changes (model versions, system prompts). For reproducibility, pin the model version, log seeds and parameters, and design evaluations that tolerate small variations.</p>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'adapt', imp: true,
    q: 'When would you use prompt engineering vs RAG vs fine-tuning?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Prompting</strong> first: cheapest, instant iteration; few-shot examples and structured output often suffice.</li><li><strong>RAG</strong> when answers need private, large or frequently updated knowledge, freshness, citations and access control — knowledge changes without retraining.</li><li><strong>Fine-tuning</strong> when you need consistent format/style/tone, a specialised skill or domain language, or to distil a large model into a smaller, cheaper one for a narrow task — with enough quality data.</li></ul><p>Fine-tuning is not a reliable way to inject facts. In practice: prompt → add RAG → fine-tune for behaviour if evals still fall short; they combine.</p>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'adapt', imp: true,
    q: 'Explain LoRA. Why does it work, and how do you choose the rank?',
    a: '<p>Freeze the pre-trained weight W and learn a low-rank update: \\(W\\prime = W + \\frac{\\alpha}{r}BA\\), with \\(A\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times d}\\), \\(B\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times r}\\), r ≪ d, and B initialised to zero so training starts from the base model. It works because the weight change needed for adaptation has low intrinsic rank. Trainable parameters drop to well under 1%, optimizer memory shrinks accordingly, adapters are small files that can be swapped per task, and they can be merged into W for zero extra latency.</p><p>Rank: start around 8–16; too low underfits complex tasks, too high wastes memory and can overfit small datasets. Target attention projections and often the MLP layers too.</p>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'adapt',
    q: 'What is QLoRA?',
    a: '<p>LoRA on top of a base model quantised to 4-bit (NF4) with double quantisation and paged optimizers. The frozen base is stored in 4 bits and de-quantised on the fly; only the LoRA adapters are trained in 16-bit. This lets you fine-tune a 30–70B model on a single large GPU with small quality loss compared with 16-bit LoRA.</p>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'adapt',
    q: 'What is catastrophic forgetting, and how do you mitigate it?',
    a: '<p>Fine-tuning on a narrow dataset can overwrite general abilities the model had (instruction following, other languages, safety behaviour). Mitigate with parameter-efficient methods (LoRA changes few parameters), lower learning rates and fewer epochs, mixing in general instruction data (replay), regularization towards the original weights, and evaluating on general benchmarks — not only the target task.</p>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'adapt',
    q: 'What prompt-engineering techniques do you use?',
    a: '<ul><li>Clear role and task, explicit constraints and output format (JSON schema / structured outputs).</li><li>Few-shot examples covering edge cases.</li><li>Asking for step-by-step reasoning (chain-of-thought) or using reasoning models for multi-step problems.</li><li>Delimiting context and instructions (XML tags), putting long documents before the question.</li><li>Decomposing into chained prompts; self-checking or verification passes.</li><li>Treating prompts as versioned code with an eval set and regression tests.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'align',
    q: 'Walk through how an LLM is trained: pre-training, SFT and RLHF.',
    a: '<ol><li><strong>Pre-training:</strong> self-supervised next-token prediction on trillions of tokens; learns language, knowledge and skills. Output: a base model.</li><li><strong>Supervised fine-tuning:</strong> train on curated instruction–response pairs so it follows instructions and adopts the assistant format.</li><li><strong>Preference alignment:</strong> RLHF — train a reward model on human preference comparisons, then optimise the policy with PPO to maximise reward with a KL penalty to the SFT model; or DPO, which optimises preferences directly without a reward model or RL. Constitutional AI / RLAIF uses AI feedback guided by written principles.</li></ol>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'align',
    q: 'DPO vs PPO-based RLHF?',
    a: '<p>PPO-based RLHF needs a separate reward model, on-policy sampling, a value function and careful tuning; it is powerful but expensive and unstable, and prone to reward hacking. DPO shows the same KL-regularised objective can be optimised with a simple classification-style loss on (chosen, rejected) pairs against a frozen reference model — no reward model or RL loop, so it is cheaper, stabler and easier to reproduce. RL-based methods remain useful with verifiable rewards (code tests, maths answers).</p>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'align', imp: true,
    q: 'Why do LLMs hallucinate, and how do you reduce it?',
    a: '<p>They are trained to produce plausible continuations, not verified facts; knowledge is compressed and imperfect, rare facts are weakly learned, training rewards confident answers, and the model can\'t tell what it doesn\'t know. Decoding randomness and long, noisy contexts add errors.</p><p><strong>Reduce:</strong> ground answers with RAG and require citations; instruct to answer only from context and to say "I don\'t know"; low temperature; constrained/structured outputs; verification steps (self-consistency, a second-pass checker, citation validation); tool use for maths and lookups; and measure faithfulness continuously in production.</p>' },
  { m: 'llm', s: 'align',
    q: 'What are scaling laws (Chinchilla) in one minute?',
    a: '<p>Loss falls predictably as a power law in model size, data and compute. Chinchilla showed many earlier models were under-trained: for a fixed compute budget, parameters and training tokens should grow roughly together (~20 tokens per parameter). Today models are often trained far beyond that ratio because inference cost favours smaller models trained on more data.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 09 · RAG ---------------- */
  { m: 'rag', s: 'pipeline', imp: true,
    q: 'What is RAG and why use it when LLMs are already powerful?',
    a: '<p>Retrieval-Augmented Generation retrieves relevant pieces of an external knowledge source at query time and puts them in the prompt so the LLM answers from them. It addresses what LLMs can\'t do alone: knowledge after the training cut-off, private/proprietary data, citations and auditability, per-user access control, and reduced hallucination by grounding. Updating knowledge means re-indexing documents, not retraining a model — cheaper and faster than fine-tuning for facts.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'pipeline', imp: true,
    q: 'Walk me through a RAG pipeline end to end.',
    a: '<p><strong>Offline indexing:</strong> ingest documents with metadata → parse (keep headings and tables) → chunk → embed → store in a vector index (plus a keyword index).</p><p><strong>Online:</strong> rewrite the query (resolve follow-ups) → embed → hybrid retrieval with metadata filters (top 20–50) → re-rank with a cross-encoder (top 3–8) → build a prompt with instructions, cited chunks and the question → generate with low temperature → return the answer with citations → log everything for evaluation and feedback.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'pipeline',
    q: 'Is RAG still relevant now that LLMs have million-token context windows?',
    a: '<p>Yes. Stuffing everything in context is slow and expensive (cost and latency scale with tokens), quality degrades with long noisy contexts ("lost in the middle"), corpora are often far larger than any window, and you still need access control, freshness and citations. Long context changes the tuning — larger chunks, more retrieved passages, whole-document retrieval for small corpora — rather than removing retrieval.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'pipeline',
    q: 'How is a RAG prompt different from a normal prompt?',
    a: '<p>It contains retrieved context with identifiers, plus grounding instructions: answer only from the context, cite sources by ID, say "I don\'t know" when the context doesn\'t contain the answer, and ignore instructions found inside documents (prompt-injection defence). Context is clearly delimited (e.g. XML tags), ordered with the most relevant chunks first, and the question usually comes after the documents.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'chunking', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you choose a chunk size and overlap?',
    a: '<p>Start from constraints: the embedding model\'s max input, the LLM context budget (k × chunk size), document structure and question type (short facts vs long explanations). A common starting point is ~300–800 tokens with 10–20% overlap, split recursively on headings → paragraphs → sentences. Then <strong>tune empirically</strong>: build a set of questions with known answer passages and measure recall@k and answer quality for several sizes. Small chunks give precise matches but lose context; large chunks keep context but blur the embedding and waste tokens.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'chunking',
    q: 'What chunking strategies do you know? Pros and cons of semantic chunking?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Fixed-size</strong> (characters/tokens): simple, cuts mid-sentence.</li><li><strong>Recursive / structure-aware</strong>: split on headings, paragraphs, sentences — the usual default.</li><li><strong>Document-specific</strong>: markdown headers, code by function, tables kept whole, PDFs by layout.</li><li><strong>Semantic</strong>: split where embedding similarity between consecutive sentences drops — coherent topics, but costs an embedding per sentence, is sensitive to thresholds and gives uneven sizes.</li><li><strong>Hierarchical / parent–child</strong>: retrieve small chunks, return their larger parent section.</li><li><strong>Contextual chunk headers</strong>: prepend the document and section title (or an LLM-written summary) to each chunk before embedding.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'chunking',
    q: 'How do you handle tables, PDFs and other structured documents?',
    a: '<p>Use layout-aware parsers (OCR for scans) to keep reading order, headings and tables. Keep each table intact as markdown/HTML or convert rows into self-contained sentences with column names; add the table caption and section as metadata. Store page numbers for citations. For heavily numeric or relational data, query a database (text-to-SQL) instead of embedding it, and route questions accordingly.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'embeddings',
    q: 'What are embeddings and how are they used in retrieval? Why cosine similarity?',
    a: '<p>Embeddings are dense vectors from an encoder trained (contrastively) so that semantically similar texts are close. At index time each chunk is embedded; at query time the question is embedded with the <em>same</em> model and the nearest chunks are retrieved. <strong>Cosine similarity</strong> compares direction and ignores magnitude (length), which is what carries meaning for most text-embedding models; with L2-normalised vectors, cosine, dot product and Euclidean distance give the same ranking, so dot product is used for speed.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'embeddings',
    q: 'How do you choose an embedding model?',
    a: '<p>Evaluate on <em>your</em> data with a labelled query set (recall@k, nDCG), using public leaderboards (MTEB retrieval tasks) only to shortlist. Consider: domain and language coverage (multilingual, code), max input length, dimension (storage and speed — Matryoshka models can be truncated), latency and cost, hosting (API vs self-hosted, data privacy), and asymmetric query/passage support. Fine-tune on in-domain pairs when generic models miss your jargon. Changing models means re-embedding the whole corpus.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'embeddings',
    q: 'How do vector databases search millions of vectors quickly? What is ANN?',
    a: '<p>Exact nearest-neighbour search compares the query with every vector — O(n·d). <strong>Approximate nearest neighbour</strong> indexes trade a little recall for orders-of-magnitude speed: <strong>HNSW</strong> builds a multi-layer proximity graph and greedily walks from coarse to fine layers (fast, high recall, memory-heavy); <strong>IVF</strong> clusters vectors and searches only the nearest clusters (nprobe); <strong>product quantisation</strong> compresses vectors into short codes to fit in memory. Tune parameters (ef_search, nprobe) for the recall/latency trade-off, and support metadata filtering.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'embeddings',
    q: 'How do you scale embeddings to very large corpora?',
    a: '<p>Smaller dimensions (Matryoshka truncation, PCA), quantisation (scalar int8 ≈ 4× smaller with little loss; binary 32× smaller, used for a fast first pass then re-scored with full vectors), sharding and replicas in the vector DB, IVF+PQ indexes, caching embeddings by content hash so only changed chunks are re-embedded, and batch/async embedding pipelines.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'search', imp: true,
    q: 'Keyword (BM25) vs semantic search — and when do you use hybrid?',
    a: '<p><strong>BM25</strong> scores term overlap with IDF weighting and length normalisation: excellent for exact identifiers (error codes, SKUs, names, acronyms), needs no training, interpretable — but blind to synonyms and paraphrases. <strong>Dense</strong> retrieval captures meaning and paraphrase but can miss exact rare tokens and out-of-domain jargon. <strong>Hybrid</strong> runs both and fuses results (Reciprocal Rank Fusion or weighted normalised scores) — the robust default for production, especially for user queries that mix natural language with codes or names.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'search',
    q: 'What is Reciprocal Rank Fusion and why is it popular?',
    a: '<p>RRF combines ranked lists by summing \\(1/(k+\\text{rank})\\) for each document across lists (k ≈ 60). It uses only ranks, so you don\'t need to normalise incomparable scores (BM25 scores vs cosine similarities); documents ranked well by several retrievers rise to the top; it is simple, robust and has a single hyperparameter.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'search',
    q: 'Sparse vs dense embeddings?',
    a: '<p><strong>Sparse</strong> vectors (BM25, SPLADE) have one dimension per vocabulary term, mostly zeros: exact term matching, interpretable (you can see which words matched), work with inverted indexes. Learned sparse models like SPLADE also add expansion terms. <strong>Dense</strong> vectors (hundreds of dimensions, all non-zero) capture semantics but are opaque and need ANN indexes.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'search',
    q: 'How do you use metadata filtering in retrieval?',
    a: '<p>Store attributes with each chunk (source, product, language, date, region, access groups) and filter at query time — pre-filtering inside the ANN search or post-filtering the candidates. It enforces permissions (users only retrieve documents they may see), handles time-sensitive questions ("latest policy"), and narrows search to the right product or region, improving both precision and safety.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'rerank', imp: true,
    q: 'Why add a re-ranker? Bi-encoder vs cross-encoder?',
    a: '<p>First-stage retrieval optimises recall cheaply but its ordering is coarse. A <strong>bi-encoder</strong> embeds query and documents independently — documents are pre-computed, so it scales to millions, but query and document never interact. A <strong>cross-encoder</strong> processes the (query, document) pair jointly with full attention and outputs a relevance score — much more accurate, but needs a forward pass per pair at query time. So: retrieve 20–100 with bi-encoder/hybrid search, re-rank with a cross-encoder (or LLM re-ranker), keep the top 3–8. It raises precision, cuts prompt tokens and noise, and lets you drop low-relevance chunks with a threshold.</p>' },
  { m: 'rag', s: 'rerank',
    q: 'Re-rankers add latency. How do you keep a RAG system fast?',
    a: '<ul><li>Re-rank fewer candidates (20 not 100), use a small distilled cross-encoder, batch pairs on GPU, or skip re-ranking for simple queries (routing).</li><li>Cache embeddings for frequent queries and cache full answers for repeated questions (semantic cache).</li><li>Run sparse and dense retrieval in parallel; tune ANN parameters.</li><li>Send fewer, shorter chunks; use a smaller/faster LLM where quality allows; stream tokens to the user.</li><li>Set per-stage latency budgets and timeouts with fallbacks.</li></ul>' },

  /* ---------------- 10 · RAG evaluation ---------------- */
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'retrieval', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you evaluate the retriever in a RAG system?',
    a: '<p>Build a labelled set of queries with their relevant chunks (from real user questions, annotated, plus LLM-generated questions per chunk, reviewed). Measure <strong>Recall@k / Hit rate@k</strong> (is the answer in what we pass to the LLM? — the most important), <strong>Precision@k</strong> (noise), and order-aware <strong>MRR</strong> and <strong>nDCG@k</strong>. Compare chunking, embedding models, hybrid weights and re-rankers on the same set, slice by query type, and re-run it on every change.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'retrieval',
    q: 'MRR vs MAP vs nDCG — when do you use each?',
    a: '<p><strong>MRR</strong> = 1/rank of the first relevant result, averaged over queries: right when one good chunk suffices (fact lookup); it ignores other relevant results. <strong>MAP</strong> averages precision at each relevant hit, so it rewards ranking <em>all</em> relevant chunks high — use when several chunks are needed. <strong>nDCG@k</strong> discounts gains by log position and supports graded relevance (highly vs partially relevant) — the standard for search ranking. If all relevant chunks are at the bottom of the list, all three are low, MRR most sharply.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'retrieval',
    q: 'Your retriever has perfect Recall@10 but low Precision@10. What problems can this cause?',
    a: '<p>The answer is in the context, but surrounded by irrelevant chunks: more prompt tokens (cost, latency), distraction and "lost in the middle" errors, contradictory snippets, and higher hallucination risk. Fix with a re-ranker and relevance threshold, smaller k for the LLM, better chunking, metadata filters, or context compression.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'retrieval',
    q: 'How do you build an evaluation dataset for RAG when you have no labels?',
    a: '<ul><li>Sample real user queries from logs; have domain experts write reference answers and mark supporting chunks.</li><li>Generate synthetic questions from chunks with an LLM (including multi-hop and unanswerable ones), then filter and review.</li><li>Cover query types: factual, comparison, procedural, numeric/table, follow-ups, out-of-scope.</li><li>Keep a held-out set; add every production failure as a regression case.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'generation', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you evaluate a RAG system end to end?',
    a: '<p>Separate the components. <strong>Retrieval:</strong> context recall and precision (Recall@k, nDCG). <strong>Generation:</strong> faithfulness/groundedness (are claims supported by the context?), answer relevance, and correctness against a reference answer. <strong>End-to-end:</strong> task success, citation accuracy, abstention on unanswerable questions, latency and cost. Use LLM-as-judge (RAGAS, TruLens, DeepEval) calibrated on human labels, offline before release and on sampled production traffic after, plus user feedback (thumbs, escalations).</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'generation',
    q: 'Faithfulness vs answer relevance vs correctness?',
    a: '<p><strong>Faithfulness</strong>: fraction of the answer\'s claims supported by the retrieved context — measures hallucination relative to the context, not truth. <strong>Answer relevance</strong>: does the answer address the question (often scored by generating questions from the answer and comparing to the original)? <strong>Correctness</strong>: agreement with a reference answer. An answer can be faithful but wrong (the context was wrong or outdated), or correct but unfaithful (the model used its own knowledge) — you need all three.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'generation', imp: true,
    q: 'How would you detect and reduce hallucinations in a production RAG system?',
    a: '<p><strong>Detect:</strong> claim-level groundedness checks with an NLI model or LLM judge against the retrieved chunks; verify every citation points to a chunk that supports the sentence; flag answers with low retrieval scores; sample traffic for human review; track user corrections.</p><p><strong>Reduce:</strong> better retrieval (recall first), fewer and cleaner chunks, explicit "answer only from context / say I don\'t know" instructions, required citations, low temperature, structured outputs, abstention thresholds, and a verification pass that removes unsupported sentences.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'generation',
    q: 'What are the pitfalls of LLM-as-a-judge?',
    a: '<p>Position and verbosity bias, self-preference (favouring its own model family\'s style), inconsistency across runs, sensitivity to prompt wording, and weak judgement on domain-specific correctness. Mitigate: clear rubrics with examples, pairwise comparisons with swapped order, reference answers, a stronger/different judge model, low temperature, and — essential — measure agreement with human labels before trusting the scores.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'debug', imp: true,
    q: 'Your RAG system retrieves the right documents but still gives wrong answers. What do you do?',
    a: '<p>It is a generation/context-assembly problem. Inspect traces: is the relevant chunk buried among many others (lost in the middle)? Are there conflicting or outdated chunks? Is the answer split across chunks or inside a badly parsed table? Is the prompt clear about grounding and citations? Fixes: re-rank and send fewer chunks with the best first, deduplicate and filter by recency, merge parent sections, tighten instructions, lower temperature, try a stronger model, and measure faithfulness before and after.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'debug',
    q: 'What are the most common reasons a RAG retriever performs poorly?',
    a: '<ul><li>Bad parsing (tables, multi-column PDFs, scanned pages).</li><li>Chunks too big, too small, or split mid-thought; no titles/context in chunks.</li><li>Embedding model mismatched to the domain/language, or mixed model versions.</li><li>Pure dense search missing exact terms (needs hybrid).</li><li>Vague or follow-up queries without rewriting.</li><li>Missing metadata filters (wrong product, old versions).</li><li>ANN parameters tuned for speed over recall.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'debug',
    q: 'How do you handle security in RAG — access control and prompt injection?',
    a: '<p><strong>Access control:</strong> attach ACL metadata to every chunk at ingestion and filter by the user\'s identity at retrieval (or keep per-tenant indexes) — never rely on prompt instructions to hide documents. <strong>Prompt injection:</strong> retrieved text is untrusted; delimit it as data, tell the model it contains no instructions, give tools least privilege and require confirmation for side effects, filter outputs, and detect injection patterns. Also redact PII before indexing where required, and audit-log what was retrieved for whom.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'debug',
    q: 'How do you keep a RAG index fresh and consistent?',
    a: '<p>Event-driven or scheduled incremental ingestion; content hashes to re-embed only changed chunks; delete on source deletion; version documents and keep only the current one (or filter by effective date); store timestamps in metadata; monitor index lag; and version the embedding model with the index so a model change triggers a full rebuild behind a blue/green switch.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'advanced', imp: true,
    q: 'What is the "lost in the middle" problem and how do you mitigate it?',
    a: '<p>LLMs use information at the beginning and end of a long context more reliably than information in the middle, and adding more retrieved passages can reduce accuracy. Mitigate by retrieving fewer, better chunks (re-rank + threshold), ordering the most relevant chunks first (or at both ends), compressing context to the relevant sentences, and breaking complex questions into sub-queries.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'advanced',
    q: 'Explain HyDE. When does it help, and what are its risks?',
    a: '<p>Hypothetical Document Embeddings: ask an LLM to write a plausible answer passage to the query, embed that passage, and search with it. Answers look more like documents than short questions do, so it bridges vocabulary and length gaps (helpful for short or jargon-light queries). Risks: an extra LLM call (latency, cost), and a hallucinated draft can steer retrieval towards wrong content — combine with the original query or use multi-query fusion.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'advanced',
    q: 'How does agentic RAG differ from standard RAG?',
    a: '<p>Standard RAG does one fixed retrieve-then-generate pass. In agentic RAG an LLM agent controls retrieval: it decides whether retrieval is needed, chooses sources or tools (vector store, SQL, web, APIs), writes and rewrites queries, grades what it got, retrieves again for missing pieces, and stops when it has enough — handling multi-hop and ambiguous questions. Costs: more latency and tokens, harder evaluation and debugging, loops — so bound steps, log traces, and evaluate trajectories as well as final answers.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'advanced',
    q: 'How do you handle ambiguous or multi-part questions in RAG?',
    a: '<p>Classify and route: ask a clarifying question when the intent is genuinely ambiguous; rewrite into a standalone query; decompose multi-part or comparison questions into sub-queries, retrieve for each, and synthesise; use multi-query generation for vague wording; and use metadata (product, region) from the conversation or user profile as filters.</p>' },
  { m: 'rageval', s: 'advanced',
    q: 'When would you use GraphRAG?',
    a: '<p>When questions depend on relationships across many documents or ask for global summaries ("what are the main themes in these 5,000 tickets?", "which suppliers are connected to this part?"). GraphRAG extracts entities and relations into a knowledge graph, builds community summaries and retrieves over that structure. It is costly to build and maintain, so use it when plain chunk retrieval clearly fails on those question types; combine with vector search for local facts.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 11 · Agents & LLMOps ---------------- */
  { m: 'agents', s: 'react', imp: true,
    q: 'What is an LLM agent? Explain the ReAct pattern.',
    a: '<p>An agent is an LLM running in a loop with access to tools and some memory, deciding its own next step towards a goal. <strong>ReAct</strong> (Reason + Act) interleaves a reasoning step ("I need the order date"), an action (a tool call), and an observation (the tool result), repeating until it can answer. Compared with chain-of-thought alone it grounds reasoning in real data; compared with a fixed pipeline it adapts to the task. Trade-offs: latency, cost, error compounding and loops — bound the steps, validate tool I/O and trace everything. If the steps are known in advance, a deterministic workflow is usually better than an agent.</p>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'react',
    q: 'Workflow vs agent — when would you not use an agent?',
    a: '<p>Use a <strong>workflow</strong> (fixed sequence of LLM calls and code: classify → retrieve → answer) when the steps are predictable: it is cheaper, faster, easier to test and debug. Use an <strong>agent</strong> when the path depends on intermediate results and can\'t be enumerated (open-ended research, multi-system troubleshooting). Many production systems are mostly workflows with an agentic step in one place.</p>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'react',
    q: 'What types of memory can an agent have?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Working / short-term</strong>: the current context window (conversation, scratchpad, tool results) — summarise or trim when it grows.</li><li><strong>Episodic</strong>: records of past interactions or task runs, retrieved when relevant.</li><li><strong>Semantic</strong>: facts and knowledge (a vector store, a knowledge base, user profile).</li><li><strong>Procedural</strong>: how to do things — system prompts, tool definitions, learned instructions or skills.</li></ul><p>Long-term memory is usually external storage plus retrieval, with care for privacy and staleness.</p>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'react',
    q: 'How do multi-agent systems work, and when do they break down?',
    a: '<p>Patterns: an orchestrator delegating to specialist sub-agents (researcher, coder, reviewer), pipelines handing work along, or debate/critique between agents. Benefits: separation of concerns, parallelism, smaller focused contexts. They break down through error propagation between agents, lost context in hand-offs, loops and runaway cost, conflicting outputs, and very hard debugging. Start with one agent and good tools; add agents only where parallelism or isolation clearly helps, with strict budgets and tracing.</p>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'tools', imp: true,
    q: 'How does function/tool calling work under the hood?',
    a: '<p>You pass tool definitions (name, description, JSON-schema parameters) with the request. The model, fine-tuned for this, emits a structured tool call instead of text when it decides a tool is needed. Your application parses and validates the arguments, executes the function under the user\'s permissions, and returns the result as a tool message; the model then continues, possibly calling more tools, until it produces a final answer. The model never executes code itself — your runtime is the trust boundary.</p>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'tools',
    q: 'How do you handle tool-call errors and retries in an agent?',
    a: '<ul><li>Validate arguments against the schema before executing; on invalid arguments, return a clear error message so the model can correct itself.</li><li>Timeouts per tool; retries with exponential backoff only for idempotent or read-only calls; idempotency keys for writes.</li><li>Structured error results (type, message, retryable) instead of stack traces.</li><li>Global step and cost budgets, loop detection, and graceful fallback ("I couldn\'t reach the order system — here is what I can tell you").</li><li>Log every call for debugging and evaluation.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'tools',
    q: 'Structured outputs vs function calling? And what is MCP?',
    a: '<p><strong>Structured outputs</strong> constrain the model\'s final answer to a JSON schema — for extraction, classification and data you consume in code, with no side effects. <strong>Function calling</strong> is for actions and data fetching the model decides to trigger mid-conversation. <strong>MCP (Model Context Protocol)</strong> is an open protocol for exposing tools, resources and prompts from a server to any compatible AI client, so integrations are written once and reused instead of hand-wiring each tool into each app.</p>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'tools',
    q: 'How do you make LLM outputs reliably parseable?',
    a: '<p>Use native structured-output / JSON-schema modes or tool calling where available; otherwise give a schema and examples, validate with Pydantic, and on failure retry with the validation error in the prompt (with a retry cap). Keep schemas small and flat, use enums for categories, set temperature low, and handle a final fallback path in code.</p>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'safety', imp: true,
    q: 'What is prompt injection, and how do you defend an agent against it?',
    a: '<p>Malicious instructions placed in the user\'s input (direct) or in content the model reads — web pages, emails, documents, tool results (indirect) — that try to override the system\'s instructions, e.g. to leak data or take actions. Defences, layered: <strong>least-privilege tools</strong> and permission checks in code; <strong>human confirmation</strong> for consequential actions; egress allow-lists; separating trusted instructions from untrusted data (delimiters, "content is data" instructions); input/output classifiers; not putting secrets in prompts; monitoring and red-teaming. Assume the model can be fooled and make sure that being fooled can\'t cause serious harm.</p>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'safety',
    q: 'What guardrails would you put around an LLM application?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Input:</strong> topic/abuse classifiers, PII detection and redaction, injection detection, rate limits.</li><li><strong>Processing:</strong> access-controlled retrieval, tool permissions, step/cost budgets.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> schema validation, groundedness/citation checks, policy and toxicity filters, PII leakage checks, safe fallbacks and escalation to humans.</li><li><strong>Ops:</strong> logging with privacy controls, red-teaming, incident review.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'serving', imp: true,
    q: 'What are the main levers for reducing LLM latency and cost?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Fewer tokens:</strong> tighter prompts, fewer/shorter retrieved chunks, capped output length, summarised history.</li><li><strong>Caching:</strong> provider prompt caching for stable prefixes; semantic caching of frequent questions; caching tool results.</li><li><strong>Model choice:</strong> route easy requests to smaller/faster models; distil or fine-tune a small model for a narrow task.</li><li><strong>Serving:</strong> streaming (perceived latency), parallel tool/retrieval calls, batch APIs for offline jobs; when self-hosting, continuous batching, quantisation, speculative decoding, KV-cache management (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM).</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'serving',
    q: 'What are continuous batching and PagedAttention?',
    a: '<p><strong>Continuous (in-flight) batching</strong> adds new requests to the running batch as soon as others finish, instead of waiting for a whole static batch to complete — keeping the GPU busy despite very different output lengths, which multiplies throughput. <strong>PagedAttention</strong> (vLLM) stores the KV cache in fixed-size blocks like virtual-memory pages, eliminating fragmentation and over-reservation, so many more sequences fit in GPU memory and shared prefixes can share blocks.</p>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'serving',
    q: 'How do you evaluate and monitor an LLM application in production?',
    a: '<p><strong>Before release:</strong> a versioned eval set (golden questions, edge cases, adversarial prompts) scored by exact checks, LLM-as-judge calibrated against humans, and human review; gate releases on regressions. <strong>In production:</strong> trace every request (prompt version, retrieved context, tool calls, outputs), track latency (TTFT, total), cost, error and refusal rates, groundedness/quality scores on sampled traffic, user feedback, and drift in query topics; A/B test prompt and model changes; treat prompts as versioned code with rollback.</p>' },
  { m: 'agents', s: 'serving',
    q: 'What is speculative decoding?',
    a: '<p>A small, fast draft model proposes several next tokens; the large target model verifies them all in a single forward pass and accepts the longest prefix that matches what it would have produced (with a rejection-sampling rule that keeps the output distribution identical). Because decoding is memory-bound, verifying k tokens costs about the same as generating one, so it gives 2–3× speed-ups when the draft model guesses well.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 12 · System design ---------------- */
  { m: 'design', s: 'framework', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you structure an answer to an ML system design question?',
    a: '<ol><li><strong>Clarify</strong> the business goal, users, scale, latency budget, constraints and what "good" means.</li><li><strong>Frame</strong> it as an ML task; choose offline metrics and online/business metrics plus guardrails.</li><li><strong>Data and labels</strong>: sources, label definition and delay, biases, leakage risks.</li><li><strong>Features</strong>, computed consistently for training and serving.</li><li><strong>Baseline first</strong> (heuristic or simple model), then the stronger model and why.</li><li><strong>Offline evaluation</strong> with a split that mimics production.</li><li><strong>Serving</strong>: batch vs real-time, architecture, latency, fallbacks.</li><li><strong>Online experiment, monitoring, retraining</strong> and iteration.</li></ol><p>Narrate trade-offs and offer a simple version 1 before the sophisticated design.</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'framework', imp: true,
    q: '"Walk me through an ML project you took from problem definition to production."',
    a: '<p>Prepare one project told in this order, with numbers: <strong>problem and why it mattered</strong> → <strong>your specific role</strong> → <strong>data and labels</strong> (how you got them, their flaws) → <strong>baseline</strong> and how you split the data → <strong>model choice and why</strong> (what you tried that failed) → <strong>evaluation</strong> offline and how you proved improvement online → <strong>deployment</strong> (serving, latency, monitoring) → <strong>outcome</strong> measured against the baseline → <strong>what you\'d do differently</strong>. Expect follow-ups: "why that model?", "what broke?", "how do you know it was the model and not seasonality?"</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'framework',
    q: 'What is training–serving skew, and how do you prevent it?',
    a: '<p>A difference between the data or code path used in training and in production, so the model sees different inputs live than it learned from: features computed by different code (SQL offline vs Java online), different freshness (daily batch vs real-time), point-in-time leakage in training, different preprocessing versions, or missing features defaulting differently. Prevent it with shared feature definitions (a feature store or one library), point-in-time correct training data, logging the exact features used at serving time and training on those logs, and shadow-mode comparisons before launch.</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'framework',
    q: 'Batch vs real-time inference — how do you choose?',
    a: '<p><strong>Batch</strong> (precompute scores nightly or hourly and store them): cheap, simple, tolerant of heavy models, but stale and can\'t use in-session context — fine for email recommendations, churn scores. <strong>Real-time</strong> (score on request): fresh context, needed for fraud at checkout, search ranking, session-aware recommendations; requires low-latency features and models, autoscaling and fallbacks. Hybrids are common: precompute candidates or embeddings in batch, re-rank in real time.</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'framework',
    q: 'What is a feature store and when do you need one?',
    a: '<p>A system that defines features once and serves them consistently for training (offline store, point-in-time correct historical values) and for inference (online store, low-latency key-value lookups), with versioning, freshness tracking and reuse across teams. It earns its overhead when many models share features, real-time features are needed, and training–serving skew has bitten you; for one batch model a well-tested SQL pipeline is enough.</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'abtest', imp: true,
    q: 'How would you design an A/B test for a new ranking model?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Hypothesis and primary metric</strong> (e.g. conversion per user) decided up front, with guardrails: latency, errors, revenue, returns, complaints.</li><li><strong>Randomisation unit</strong>: users (sticky assignment), not requests, so experiences are consistent; check for interference (shared inventory, marketplaces, store staff) and use cluster or switchback designs if needed.</li><li><strong>Sample size and duration</strong> from baseline rate, MDE, α and power; run whole weeks.</li><li><strong>Validity checks</strong>: A/A test, sample-ratio mismatch, logging parity.</li><li><strong>Analysis</strong>: no peeking (or use sequential tests), segment slices, novelty effects; ramp gradually and keep a holdout.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'abtest',
    q: 'What is "peeking" and why is it a problem?',
    a: '<p>Checking the p-value repeatedly during a test and stopping as soon as it crosses 0.05. Each look is another chance for noise to cross the threshold, so the real false-positive rate can be several times higher than 5%. Fix: fix the sample size in advance and analyse once, or use sequential testing methods (alpha spending, always-valid p-values) designed for continuous monitoring.</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'abtest',
    q: 'What is sample-ratio mismatch?',
    a: '<p>When the observed split between arms differs significantly from the designed split (e.g. 50.8/49.2 with millions of users, chi-square p &lt; 0.001). It signals a bug — assignment, redirects, crashes or logging that drops users in one arm — which biases results. Results from a test with SRM shouldn\'t be trusted until the cause is found.</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'abtest',
    q: 'How do you reduce the time an A/B test needs?',
    a: '<p>Variance reduction with pre-experiment covariates (CUPED), more sensitive proxy metrics that correlate with the goal (add-to-cart instead of purchase), larger traffic allocation, triggering analysis only on users who actually saw the change, and interleaving for ranking changes (both rankers\' results mixed in one list — far more sensitive than A/B).</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'offline', imp: true,
    q: 'Offline metrics improved but the online A/B test got worse. What would you investigate?',
    a: '<ol><li><strong>Experiment validity</strong>: SRM, randomisation unit, duration/power, novelty, bot traffic.</li><li><strong>Logging and metric parity</strong> between arms.</li><li><strong>Training–serving skew</strong>: features missing or stale online, wrong model version.</li><li><strong>Offline leakage</strong>: post-event features, random splits.</li><li><strong>System effects</strong>: higher latency or timeouts, stale inventory surfaced.</li><li><strong>Objective mismatch</strong>: trained on clicks while the goal is purchases or kept purchases.</li><li><strong>Selection/position bias</strong>: offline labels only cover items the old system showed.</li><li><strong>Diversity/feedback loops and segment effects.</strong></li></ol><p>Then align the label with the goal, add guardrails, and use interleaving or counterfactual evaluation before the next test.</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'offline',
    q: 'How can you evaluate a new ranking policy offline without the bias of logged data?',
    a: '<p>Logged data only shows outcomes for what the old policy chose. Use <strong>counterfactual / off-policy evaluation</strong>: inverse propensity scoring (weight each logged outcome by new-policy probability ÷ logging probability — needs logged propensities, e.g. from randomised exploration), doubly robust estimators, or replay on randomised traffic. Online, <strong>interleaving</strong> gives fast, sensitive comparisons before a full A/B test.</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'monitor', imp: true,
    q: 'Model performance dropped in production. How do you debug it?',
    a: '<ol><li><strong>Rule out bugs first</strong>: pipeline failures, schema/unit changes, null spikes, feature freshness, a bad deploy (compare model version, check training–serving skew via logged features).</li><li><strong>Characterise the drop</strong>: when it started, which segments, which metrics; correlate with releases, campaigns, seasonality, upstream changes.</li><li><strong>Data drift</strong> (input distributions, PSI/KS) vs <strong>concept drift</strong> (same inputs, different outcomes — check performance on fresh labels) vs label/prior shift.</li><li><strong>Fix</strong>: repair the pipeline, retrain on recent data, recalibrate, add features for the new pattern, or adjust thresholds; then add a monitor so it is caught earlier next time.</li></ol>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'monitor',
    q: 'Data drift vs concept drift — how do you detect each?',
    a: '<p><strong>Data drift</strong>: P(X) changes. Detect without labels by comparing feature and prediction distributions to a reference window: PSI, KS test, Jensen–Shannon distance, embedding drift, new-category rates. <strong>Concept drift</strong>: P(y|X) changes. Detect with labels — performance on recent labelled data, delayed-label metrics, calibration over time, proxy business KPIs. Data drift doesn\'t always hurt performance, so alert on drift but decide on performance.</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'monitor', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you deploy, monitor and safely update a model?',
    a: '<p>Version model, data snapshot, features and code together; gate releases on an offline evaluation suite (overall and slices, calibration, latency). Roll out via <strong>shadow</strong> (score live traffic, discard outputs) → <strong>canary</strong> (small traffic share with automatic guardrail rollback) → <strong>A/B</strong> → full rollout, keeping the previous model warm for instant rollback. Monitor system (latency, errors), data (freshness, drift) and model/business metrics; retrain on schedule or on triggers through the same gates.</p>' },
  { m: 'design', s: 'monitor',
    q: 'A downstream ML service (e.g. a personalisation API or an LLM) goes down. What does the user see?',
    a: '<p>Design for it: strict <strong>timeouts</strong> inside the latency budget, bounded <strong>retries</strong> with backoff only where idempotent and time allows, a <strong>circuit breaker</strong> that stops calling a failing dependency and fails fast, and a <strong>fallback</strong> that still satisfies hard constraints — e.g. cached last-known recommendations, or popular, in-stock items for the user\'s segment. Serve stale cache with a freshness limit, log fallback rates as a monitored metric, and degrade gracefully rather than showing errors or empty pages.</p>' },

  /* ---------------- 13 · Recommender systems ---------------- */
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'funnel', imp: true,
    q: 'Design a product recommendation system for an e-commerce app with physical stores.',
    a: '<p><strong>Clarify:</strong> surfaces (home feed, product page "similar", post-purchase), objective (purchases net of returns, not clicks), constraints (inventory per store and online, latency ~200 ms p95, cold start), data (views, try-ons, carts, orders, returns, product attributes and images, store stock).</p><p><strong>Architecture:</strong> candidate generation from several sources (two-tower ANN, co-viewed/co-bought, similar to recently viewed, trending by city) → hard filters (in stock at the selected store or deliverable, eligible, not already bought) → ranking model (GBDT or multi-task deep model predicting click, try-on, purchase) with user, item, context and cross features → re-ranking for diversity, business rules and exploration → fallback to popular in-stock items.</p><p><strong>Evaluate</strong> offline with time-based splits (Recall@K for retrieval, NDCG@K for ranking), online with A/B tests on conversion and revenue net of returns with latency, coverage and return-rate guardrails. <strong>v1:</strong> popularity by segment + co-purchase + stock filter + a simple ranker, then iterate.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'funnel',
    q: 'The recommendation API must meet a 200 ms p95 latency target. How would you design it?',
    a: '<ul><li><strong>Budget per stage</strong> (network, retrieval, features, filtering, ranking, re-ranking) with timeouts for each.</li><li><strong>Precompute</strong> item embeddings, item features and heavy candidate lists offline; ANN indexes in memory; cache user features per session.</li><li><strong>Parallel</strong> calls to retrieval sources and feature stores; batch feature lookups.</li><li><strong>Bounded candidate sets</strong> (e.g. 500–1000), two-stage ranking (cheap pre-ranker, then heavy ranker on top 100), batched model inference.</li><li><strong>Keep slow work off the path</strong>: LLM-generated explanations asynchronous or precomputed.</li><li><strong>Fallbacks</strong> when a stage times out: cached results or popular in-stock items; monitor p95/p99 and fallback rate.</li></ul>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'funnel', imp: true,
    q: 'How do you evaluate a recommender before and after launch? What do Precision@K, Recall@K and NDCG@K miss?',
    a: '<p><strong>Offline:</strong> time-based split (train on the past, test on the next period). Retrieval: Recall@K / hit rate (did the item the user later engaged with appear in the candidates?). Ranking: NDCG@K and MAP (order matters), Precision@K. Also coverage, diversity, novelty, calibration of predicted probabilities. <strong>What they miss:</strong> they only judge items the old system exposed (selection and position bias), ignore latency, inventory and returns, and reward relevance over discovery.</p><p><strong>Online:</strong> A/B test with conversion, revenue per user, purchases net of returns as primary metrics; guardrails on latency, error/fallback rate, return rate, catalogue coverage; interleaving for faster ranker comparisons; long-term holdouts.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'funnel', imp: true,
    q: 'Where would an LLM improve recommendations, and when would you leave it out?',
    a: '<p><strong>Useful:</strong> understanding free-text or conversational queries ("light frames for a round face, under 3k"), enriching item metadata and tags from descriptions and images, generating short explanations, cold-start item understanding, and re-ranking a small candidate list with rich reasoning.</p><p><strong>Leave it out of the hot path</strong> when the ranker already works: LLMs add latency and cost, can invent product IDs or out-of-stock items, return invalid output and rank inconsistently. If used, give it a bounded candidate set of real IDs, validate outputs against the catalogue and stock, set timeouts with a non-LLM fallback, cache results, and prove incremental value with an A/B test.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'models', imp: true,
    q: 'Explain collaborative filtering, content-based recommendation and a hybrid approach.',
    a: '<p><strong>Collaborative filtering</strong> uses only interaction patterns (user–item matrix): neighbourhood methods or matrix factorisation, which learns user and item vectors whose dot product predicts interaction. Captures taste that attributes don\'t describe; fails for new users/items and is biased towards popular items. <strong>Content-based</strong> recommends items similar in attributes or embeddings (shape, colour, price, image) to what the user engaged with; handles new items, but can be narrow and needs good features. <strong>Hybrid</strong> combines both — e.g. a two-tower model whose item tower uses ID embeddings plus attributes and image embeddings, or a ranker that takes CF scores and content features as inputs.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'models', imp: true,
    q: 'What is a two-tower model? Inputs, training objective and limitations?',
    a: '<p>Two neural encoders: a <strong>user/query tower</strong> (user ID, history, demographics, context such as time, device, city) and an <strong>item tower</strong> (item ID, category, price, attributes, image/text embeddings), each producing a vector; relevance = dot product or cosine. Trained on positive (user, item) pairs with sampled-softmax / contrastive loss using in-batch or sampled negatives (with popularity correction). Item vectors are precomputed into an ANN index, so retrieval over millions of items is milliseconds. <strong>Limitations:</strong> no feature interaction between user and item before the dot product (less precise than a ranker), sensitive to negative sampling and popularity bias, needs retraining as items change. Hence it is used for retrieval, with a richer ranker afterwards.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'models',
    q: 'How does matrix factorisation work, and when is a simpler model enough?',
    a: '<p>Factorise the sparse user–item matrix R ≈ PQᵀ, with k-dimensional user vectors P and item vectors Q, by minimising squared error on observed entries plus L2 regularization (SGD or ALS); implicit-feedback versions weight observed interactions by confidence. Predictions for unseen pairs are the dot products. A simpler model is enough when the catalogue is small, data is sparse, or the surface is simple: popularity by segment, co-purchase ("frequently bought together") and item-to-item similarity are strong baselines that every complex model must beat.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'models', imp: true,
    q: 'How would you recommend items to a new user? What about a newly launched item?',
    a: '<p><strong>User cold start:</strong> popular and trending items for the segment we do know (city, device, referral source, entry page); a short onboarding (style, face shape, budget); session-based recommendations that update after every click or try-on; contextual bandits to explore efficiently; move to personalised models as history accumulates.</p><p><strong>Item cold start:</strong> content features and visual/text embeddings place the new item near similar items in the item tower; inherit signals from brand/collection; guaranteed exploration slots or a boost for a limited time; track its early CTR with a bandit. Always fall back to sensible popular, in-stock items.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'labels', imp: true,
    q: 'What would count as positive and negative training examples?',
    a: '<p><strong>Positives</strong> depend on the objective: clicks (plentiful, noisy), try-ons or add-to-cart (stronger intent), purchases (closest to value, sparse), kept purchases (net of returns, delayed). Often a multi-task model predicts several and the final score combines them.</p><p><strong>Negatives:</strong> items shown but not engaged with (real exposure; weight by position since low slots were barely seen). An item the user never saw is <em>not</em> a genuine negative — random or in-batch negatives are a practical approximation for retrieval training, with popularity correction, plus hard negatives (shown, similar, not chosen) for rankers. Avoid leakage: point-in-time features, time-based splits, no post-impression signals.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'labels',
    q: 'What is position bias and how do you correct for it?',
    a: '<p>Items in higher slots get more clicks regardless of relevance, so logged clicks over-credit top positions and the model learns to reproduce the old ranking. Corrections: include position as a feature during training and set it to a fixed value at serving; inverse propensity weighting using click-propensity estimates per position (from randomisation experiments); a separate position-bias tower (shallow tower) whose output is dropped at inference; and evaluation with interleaving.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'labels',
    q: 'Implicit vs explicit feedback?',
    a: '<p><strong>Explicit</strong>: ratings, likes, reviews — clear preference but rare and biased towards strong opinions. <strong>Implicit</strong>: views, clicks, dwell time, try-ons, purchases — abundant but noisy, only positive (absence isn\'t dislike), and confounded by exposure. Most production recommenders train on implicit feedback with confidence weighting and careful negative sampling.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'blend', imp: true,
    q: 'How would you combine several signals — fit/compatibility, purchase likelihood, popularity, availability?',
    a: '<p>Separate <strong>constraints</strong> from <strong>objectives</strong>. Availability (in stock at the user\'s store or deliverable), eligibility and prescription compatibility are hard filters — never weights. For soft signals, put them on a comparable scale (calibrated probabilities or normalised scores) because raw scales differ wildly (popularity counts vs probabilities). Start with a weighted sum or an expected-value formula (e.g. P(purchase) × margin × fit adjustment) with hand-set weights; better, feed the signals as features into a ranker trained on the true outcome (purchase net of returns) so the trade-off is learned; tune remaining business weights by A/B test.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'blend',
    q: 'What if the most relevant product is unavailable?',
    a: '<p>Don\'t show it as a buyable recommendation. Filter it from the main slate and offer the closest in-stock alternative (nearest neighbour in embedding/attribute space), show availability at a nearby store or for home delivery, or offer a back-in-stock alert. Log these cases: frequent "best item unavailable" events are a demand signal for inventory planning. Make sure stock data is fresh — a stale cache that shows sold-out items erodes trust.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'blend',
    q: 'How would you build an image-based fit or compatibility score (e.g. face shape to frame)?',
    a: '<p>Pipeline: face detection and quality checks (blur, lighting, pose) → landmarks or a face embedding → a model predicting compatibility with each frame\'s attributes or embedding. Labels from stylist annotations, try-on outcomes and purchases-kept, with care for bias (evaluate across skin tones, ages, genders and devices). When confidence is low (bad image, extreme pose), fall back to non-visual recommendations instead of a confident wrong answer. Treat the score as one ranking feature, process images with explicit consent, and minimise storage.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'explore', imp: true,
    q: 'The recommender keeps showing bestsellers. How would you improve discovery?',
    a: '<p>It\'s a feedback loop: exposure creates clicks, clicks create exposure, and new or niche items never get a chance. Fixes: <strong>exploration</strong> — bandits (ε-greedy, UCB, Thompson sampling, contextual bandits) on a slice of traffic or slots; <strong>de-biasing</strong> — popularity correction in retrieval training (logQ), position-bias correction, down-weighting popularity features; <strong>diversity</strong> in re-ranking (MMR, category caps); <strong>new-item boosts</strong>; and measuring coverage, novelty and long-tail exposure alongside conversion. Choose the reward carefully (clicks are fast but noisy; purchases are what matter).</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'explore',
    q: 'Explain Thompson sampling. How does it compare with ε-greedy?',
    a: '<p>Keep a posterior over each option\'s reward rate (e.g. Beta(1 + clicks, 1 + non-clicks)); each time, sample a value from every posterior and choose the highest sample; update with the observed reward. Uncertain options occasionally sample high and get tried; clearly bad options rarely do — exploration fades naturally as evidence accumulates. ε-greedy explores uniformly at random a fixed ε of the time forever, wasting traffic on known-bad options, and ignores uncertainty. Contextual bandits extend this to personalised choices using features.</p>' },
  { m: 'recsys', s: 'explore',
    q: 'How do you add diversity to a recommendation list?',
    a: '<p>Re-rank with <strong>Maximal Marginal Relevance</strong>: repeatedly pick the item maximising λ·relevance − (1−λ)·max similarity to items already chosen; or apply category/brand caps, determinantal point processes, or sliding-window rules. Measure intra-list diversity, coverage and session-level engagement — and A/B test, since too much diversity hurts relevance.</p>' },
];
